@lang en

-- us-interpretation-time.l4
-- Row legalese-sonnet-5.5, Title 1 of the United States Code (General Provisions), release point Public Law 119-121.
-- Source: usc01.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter script. It is NOT the official text,
-- and nothing here is "verified against the official text".
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS FOR
-- Other encodings need an executable answer to: when does an enactment come into force or begin to bind;
-- how is a period of days, weeks, months or years counted; what happens when the last day is a day of rest,
-- recess or holiday; when is a document treated as served; what do "day", "week", "month" and "year" mean.
-- Those answers belong in an interpretation provision. Title 1 is where one would look.
--
-- WHAT THE TEXT ANSWERS: NONE OF THEM.
-- All 39 sections were searched (ss 1-8, 101-114 with 106a, 106b, 112a and 112b, and 201-213) for
-- day, week, month, year, holiday, Sunday, Saturday, recess, adjourn, commence, effective, in force, serve,
-- notice, deemed, hour and forthwith. The text has no rule on any of the six items. So Part A below
-- is a set of named REFUSEs, one for each item. Each refusal says what is missing, and no refusal is a default.
-- A caller that needs a number from Title 1 gets a refusal. Do not paper over it with `daydate`:
-- `add months` clamps a start on the 31st to the last day of a shorter month and `add years` clamps
-- 29 February to 28 February (measured, see NOTES.md), but Title 1 says neither, so neither is used in Part A.
--
-- DAYS OF REST, RECESS AND HOLIDAY ARE AN INPUT, NEVER COMPUTED.
-- Every function that could use them takes them as a LIST OF DATE that the caller supplies. No calendar is hard-coded.
--
-- PART B holds the few times and periods that Title 1's own operative sections state ("the last six days of a
-- session", "the day preceding the commencement of the session", "N days after the date on which ...").
-- They are NOT interpretation rules. They take the words of the section in their ordinary sense
-- ("N days after D" is D plus N days). Each carries its own @ref and each is a fork in NOTES.md.
--
-- Imports: prelude and daydate only.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `Part A — what Title 1 says about counting time, commencement, service and time words: nothing`

DECLARE `Unit of a period` IS ONE OF
    days
    weeks
    months
    years

DECLARE `A period` HAS
    `how many`   IS A NUMBER
    `counted in` IS A `Unit of a period`

DECLARE `What an instrument says about its own commencement` IS ONE OF
    `it says nothing about when it comes into force`
    `it fixes a day` HAS `the day it fixes` IS A DATE

DECLARE `A time word` IS ONE OF
    `the word day`
    `the word week`
    `the word month`
    `the word year`
    `the words calendar year`
    `the word session`
    `the word forthwith`

§§ `A.1 — the refusals, one named definition per missing rule`

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on counting a period of days
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on counting a period of weeks
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of weeks from a day` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of weeks from a day"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on counting a period of months
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of months after an event` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on counting a period of years
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of years after an event` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on a last day that is a day of rest, recess or holiday
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 has no rule for a last day that is a day of rest, recess or holiday` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 has no rule for a last day that is a day of rest, recess or holiday"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on counting, so none on whether a date is inside a period
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Title 1 has no counting rule, so it cannot say whether a date falls inside a period` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 has no counting rule, so it cannot say whether a date falls inside a period"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on the day from which an Act binds; s 106a says only that a bill "becomes a law or takes effect"
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 does not say from what day an Act binds when the Act does not say` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 does not say from what day an Act binds when the Act does not say"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on the moment of a day from which an Act binds
GIVETH A TIME
`Title 1 does not say from what moment of its commencement day an Act binds` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 does not say from what moment of its commencement day an Act binds"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on when subordinate legislation comes into force
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 does not say when subordinate legislation comes into force when it does not say` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 does not say when subordinate legislation comes into force when it does not say"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on deemed service or notice
GIVETH A DATE
`Title 1 has no rule on when a document is treated as served or a notice as given` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 has no rule on when a document is treated as served or a notice as given"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): the word "day" is used (ss 106, 112b, 204) and never defined
GIVETH A STRING
`Title 1 gives no meaning to the word day` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 gives no meaning to the word day"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): the word "week" does not occur in the text
GIVETH A STRING
`Title 1 gives no meaning to the word week` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 gives no meaning to the word week"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): the word "month" is used (s 112b(a)(1)) and never defined
GIVETH A STRING
`Title 1 gives no meaning to the word month` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 gives no meaning to the word month"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): the word "year" is used (ss 105, 112b, 202) and never defined
GIVETH A STRING
`Title 1 gives no meaning to the word year` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 gives no meaning to the word year"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): "calendar year" is used (ss 105, 112a(a)) and never defined
GIVETH A STRING
`Title 1 gives no meaning to the words calendar year` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 gives no meaning to the words calendar year"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): "session" is used (ss 106, 112, 204) and never defined
GIVETH A STRING
`Title 1 gives no meaning to the word session` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 gives no meaning to the word session"

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): "forthwith" is used (ss 106a, 106b) and never defined
GIVETH A STRING
`Title 1 gives no meaning to the word forthwith` MEANS
    REFUSE "Title 1 gives no meaning to the word forthwith"

§§ `A.2 — the six items, as named functions`

-- Item 1. The last day of a period of N days or N weeks counted from a day.
-- Forks the text would have to settle, and does not: whether the start day counts as day 1 or day 0;
-- whether "from" is the same as "after"; whether the last day is inside the period.
@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on counting a period of days or weeks
GIVEN period IS A `A period`
      `the day it is counted from` IS A DATE
      `the days of rest, recess or holiday the caller supplies` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of days or weeks counted from a day` MEANS
    CONSIDER period's `counted in`
    WHEN days   THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day`
    WHEN weeks  THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of weeks from a day`
    WHEN months THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of months after an event`
    WHEN years  THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of years after an event`

-- Item 2. The last day of a period of N months or N years counted after an event.
-- Forks the text would have to settle, and does not: calendar months or fixed-length months;
-- a start on the 31st; a start on 29 February; a month that lacks the number of the start day.
@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on counting a period of months or years
GIVEN period IS A `A period`
      `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the days of rest, recess or holiday the caller supplies` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of months or years counted after an event` MEANS
    CONSIDER period's `counted in`
    WHEN days   THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day`
    WHEN weeks  THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of weeks from a day`
    WHEN months THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of months after an event`
    WHEN years  THEN `Title 1 has no rule for counting a period of years after an event`

-- Item 3. A period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday.
-- Forks the text would have to settle, and does not: whether the rule applies to periods in months and years
-- or only to periods in days; and what it does when several last days in a row are days of rest.
@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on a last day that is a day of rest, recess or holiday
GIVEN period IS A `A period`
      `the last day as counted` IS A DATE
      `the days of rest, recess or holiday the caller supplies` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period once days of rest, recess or holiday are allowed for` MEANS
    `Title 1 has no rule for a last day that is a day of rest, recess or holiday`

-- Item 4. Whether a given date falls inside a period.
-- When the period is stated as N units counted from a day or after an event, the answer needs the counting rule
-- Title 1 lacks, so it refuses.
@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no counting rule
GIVEN `the date asked about` IS A DATE
      period IS A `A period`
      `the day it is counted from or the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the days of rest, recess or holiday the caller supplies` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a date falls inside a period counted from a day or after an event` MEANS
    `Title 1 has no counting rule, so it cannot say whether a date falls inside a period`

-- Item 4, the one part that is arithmetic and not a rule. When the caller already holds BOTH ends of a span,
-- this says whether a date lies on or between them. No section of Title 1 governs it, so it carries no @ref to a rule.
-- "On or between" is what this function is named for; it is not a reading of any section.
GIVEN `the date asked about` IS A DATE
      `the first day given` IS A DATE
      `the last day given` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a date lies on or between the first day given and the last day given` MEANS
        `the date asked about` AT LEAST `the first day given`
    AND `the date asked about` AT MOST  `the last day given`

-- Item 5. The day, and the moment within the day, from which an Act binds.
-- Where the Act fixes a day itself, that day is the Act's own word and Title 1 neither supports nor displaces it:
-- the answer is the day the Act fixed. Where the Act says nothing, Title 1 does not fill the gap. The moment within
-- the day is never answered, because no section says.
@ref 1 U.S.C. s 106a (a bill "becomes a law or takes effect"; no day or moment is given), and ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 otherwise silent
GIVEN `the Act's own commencement provision` IS A `What an instrument says about its own commencement`
      `the day the Act is passed or published` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day from which an Act binds` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the Act's own commencement provision`
    WHEN `it fixes a day` fixed THEN fixed
    WHEN `it says nothing about when it comes into force` THEN
        `Title 1 does not say from what day an Act binds when the Act does not say`

@ref 1 U.S.C. s 106a (a bill "becomes a law or takes effect"; no day or moment is given), and ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 otherwise silent
GIVEN `the Act's own commencement provision` IS A `What an instrument says about its own commencement`
      `the day the Act is passed or published` IS A DATE
GIVETH A TIME
`the moment within its commencement day from which an Act binds` MEANS
    `Title 1 does not say from what moment of its commencement day an Act binds`

-- Item 6. The day subordinate legislation (regulations, rules, orders) comes into force.
@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on subordinate legislation
GIVEN `the instrument's own commencement provision` IS A `What an instrument says about its own commencement`
      `the day the instrument is made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day from which subordinate legislation comes into force` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the instrument's own commencement provision`
    WHEN `it fixes a day` fixed THEN fixed
    WHEN `it says nothing about when it comes into force` THEN
        `Title 1 does not say when subordinate legislation comes into force when it does not say`

-- Deemed service or notice (must-encode item 4 of the brief).
@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no rule on deemed service or notice
GIVEN `the day the document is sent or the notice is given` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day a document is treated as served` MEANS
    `Title 1 has no rule on when a document is treated as served or a notice as given`

-- The meanings of the time words (must-encode item 6 of the brief).
@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 1-8, 101-114, 201-213 (every section searched): no time word is defined
GIVEN word IS A `A time word`
GIVETH A STRING
`the meaning Title 1 gives to a time word` MEANS
    CONSIDER word
    WHEN `the word day`           THEN `Title 1 gives no meaning to the word day`
    WHEN `the word week`          THEN `Title 1 gives no meaning to the word week`
    WHEN `the word month`         THEN `Title 1 gives no meaning to the word month`
    WHEN `the word year`          THEN `Title 1 gives no meaning to the word year`
    WHEN `the words calendar year` THEN `Title 1 gives no meaning to the words calendar year`
    WHEN `the word session`       THEN `Title 1 gives no meaning to the word session`
    WHEN `the word forthwith`     THEN `Title 1 gives no meaning to the word forthwith`

§ `Part B — times and periods that Title 1's own operative sections state`

-- Everything in Part B takes the words of one section in their ordinary sense. None of it is an interpretation
-- provision, none of it is cited by Part A, and each use is a fork in NOTES.md.
-- A "day" here is a calendar day. Title 1 defines no day, so that too is ordinary meaning (fork F-time-word).

§§ `B.1 — section 106: "the last six days of a session"`

-- src:86 | During the last six days of a session such engrossing and enrolling of bills and joint resolutions may be done otherwise than as above prescribed, upon the order of Congress by concurrent resolution.
@ref 1 U.S.C. s 106, last sentence (src:86)
GIVEN `the last day of the session` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first of the last six days of a session` MEANS
    `the last day of the session` MINUS 5

-- "The last six days" are the last day of the session and the five before it. A session's last day is a day of
-- the session, so it is inside the six; a day after the session has ended is not a day of the session.
@ref 1 U.S.C. s 106, last sentence (src:86)
GIVEN `the date asked about` IS A DATE
      `the last day of the session` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a date falls in the last six days of a session` MEANS
    `a date lies on or between the first day given and the last day given`
        `the date asked about`
        (`the first of the last six days of a session` `the last day of the session`)
        `the last day of the session`

§§ `B.2 — section 204(a) and (b): "the day preceding the commencement of the session following the last session"`

-- src:237 | The matter set forth in the edition of the Code of Laws of the United States current at any time shall, together with the then current supplement, if any, establish prima facie the laws of the United States, general and permanent in their nature, in force on the day preceding the commencement of the session following the last session the legislation of which is included
-- src:239 | The matter set forth in the edition of the Code of the District of Columbia current at any time shall, together with the then current supplement, if any, establish prima facie the laws, general and permanent in their nature, relating to or in force in the District of Columbia on the day preceding the commencement of the session following the last session the legislation of which is included
@ref 1 U.S.C. s 204(a) and (b) (src:237 src:239)
GIVEN `the day the following session commences` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day as at which a Code establishes the law` MEANS
    `the day the following session commences` MINUS 1

§§ `B.3 — section 112b and section 202(c): "N days after the date on which", "N years after the date of"`

-- Title 1 has no counting rule (Part A). Section 112b says "not later than 120 days after the date on which an
-- international agreement enters into force", and the same shape for 30, 15 and 3. The words themselves put the
-- date on which the event happened BEFORE the period: the first day counted is the day after it. This is the
-- ordinary meaning of "after", not a rule of Title 1. A reader who holds that any counting needs a rule
-- should treat every function in B.3 as a refusal; that is the alternative reading in NOTES.md, F-after.
GIVEN `the number of days` IS A NUMBER
      `the date on which the event happens` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day that is a number of days after a date` MEANS
    `the date on which the event happens` PLUS `the number of days`

@ref 1 U.S.C. ss 112b(b)(1), (b)(2), (c), (d), (h) and 202(c): the same calendar date a number of years later
GIVETH A DATE
`the same calendar date does not exist that many years after the date` MEANS
    REFUSE "the same calendar date does not exist that many years after the date, and Title 1 does not say what then"

-- "N years after D" is taken as the same calendar date N years later. Where that date does not exist (a start
-- on 29 February, N years later in a year that has no 29 February) the ordinary meaning stops and Title 1 supplies
-- nothing, so the answer is a refusal. `add years` is used ONLY when it has not clamped.
GIVEN `the number of years` IS A NUMBER
      `the date on which the event happens` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day that is a number of years after a date` MEANS
    IF       DATE_DAY   (`add years` `the date on which the event happens` `the number of years`)
             EQUALS DATE_DAY `the date on which the event happens`
         AND DATE_MONTH (`add years` `the date on which the event happens` `the number of years`)
             EQUALS DATE_MONTH `the date on which the event happens`
    THEN `add years` `the date on which the event happens` `the number of years`
    ELSE `the same calendar date does not exist that many years after the date`

§§ `B.4 — section 112b(b)(2): "during the preceding 120 days"`

-- "Not less frequently than once every 120 days, the Secretary shall make the text of each qualifying non-binding
-- instrument that became operative during the preceding 120 days ... available". The preceding 120 days are the
-- 120 days that come before the day of the posting: the last of them is the day before it, and the day of the
-- posting is not one of them. That is the ordinary meaning of "preceding", not a rule of Title 1.
-- src:140 | Not less frequently than once every 120 days, the Secretary shall make the text of each qualifying non-binding instrument that became operative during the preceding 120 days
@ref 1 U.S.C. s 112b(b)(2) (src:140)
GIVEN `the date asked about` IS A DATE
      `the day of the posting` IS A DATE
      `the number of days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a date falls in the preceding number of days` MEANS
    `a date lies on or between the first day given and the last day given`
        `the date asked about`
        (`the day of the posting` MINUS `the number of days`)
        (`the day of the posting` MINUS 1)

§§ `B.5 — section 112b(h)(1): "the 9-year period beginning on the date of the enactment of this section"`

-- A period "beginning on" a day includes that day, and a period of N years lasts until the day before the day
-- N years after it (a period of 9 years beginning on 2020-03-01 ends on 2029-02-28). Again the ordinary meaning;
-- where the same calendar date N years later does not exist, the answer is the same refusal as in B.3.
-- src:162 | and not less frequently than once every 3 years thereafter during the 9-year period beginning on the date of the enactment of this section, the Comptroller General of the United States shall conduct an audit of the compliance of the Secretary with the requirements of this section.
@ref 1 U.S.C. s 112b(h)(1) (src:162)
GIVEN `the date asked about` IS A DATE
      `the day the period begins` IS A DATE
      `the number of years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a date falls in the period of a number of years beginning on a day` MEANS
        `the date asked about` AT LEAST `the day the period begins`
    AND `the date asked about` LESS THAN (`the day that is a number of years after a date` `the number of years` `the day the period begins`)
